Z||000013||LarryB||06-27-2001||06:49 PM||||Detroit has largely woken up to the 'bigger is better' fallacy; unlike the Japanese they refuse to adequately fund research design and build to the latest technologies because the feel its cheaper to stick with what they consider tried and true.  That's why companies like Honda a) spend millions in research yearly, using different materials, trying things like oval pistons, etc. etc. and b) sell cars reliable and inexpensive enough for the mass market that in many cases offer performance way beyond most offerings from the US manufacturers. <P>(Honda's a pretty good example because a lot of their initial designs are first tried out on motorcycle engines...if the technology is promising they'll try it on car engines as well)<P>||||reg||1
